reasoning:
The vulnerability: `entry_id = *buf++;` with no check `entry_id < 16` for clut4 or `< 16` for clut16, etc. The arrays are:

Let me check DVBSubCLUT struct:

```c
typedef struct DVBSubCLUT {
    int id;
    int version;

    uint32_t clut4[4];
    uint32_t clut16[16];
    uint32_t clut256[256];

    struct DVBSubCLUT *next;
} DVBSubCLUT;
```

So entry_id up to 255. clut4[entry_id] with entry_id up to 255 writes out of bounds (into clut16 area if entry_id < 20, but beyond struct for entry_id >= 4+16+256... wait clut4 is first: writing clut4[entry_id] with entry_id up to 255 overwrites clut16 and clut256 and even next pointer and beyond the struct (heap overflow). clut16[entry_id] with entry_id up to 255 overwrites clut256 and next pointer and beyond. clut256[entry_id] max 255 so it's fine.

So the fix upstream was:

```c
        if (depth & 0x80)
            clut->clut4[entry_id] = RGBA(r,g,b,255 - alpha);
        else if (depth & 0x40)
            clut->clut16[entry_id] = RGBA(r,g,b,255 - alpha);
        else if (depth & 0x20)
            clut->clut256[entry_id] = RGBA(r,g,b,255 - alpha);
```

Wait, the actual upstream fix (commit "avcodec/dvbsubdec: Check entry_id") added:

```c
        if (depth & 0x80 && entry_id < 4)
            clut->clut4[entry_id] = ...
        else if (depth & 0x40 && entry_id < 16)
            ...
        else if (depth & 0x20 && entry_id < 256)
```

Hmm, actually the fix was:

```c
        if (entry_id <  4 && depth & 0x80)
            clut->clut4[entry_id] = RGBA(r,g,b,255 - alpha);
        else if (entry_id < 16 && depth&0x40)
            clut->clut16[entry_id] = RGBA(r,g,b,255 - alpha);
        else if (entry_id < 256 && depth&0x20)
            clut->clut256[entry_id] = RGBA(r,g,b,255 - alpha);
```

Yes, that's commit 17fb9d7cb7cab80684ec9f440f198f3cd9032c6c or similar. Anyway.

Now I need to construct a DVB subtitle packet stream that:
1. Gets parsed by dvbsubdec.
2. Contains a CLUT segment with entry_id >= 4 with depth 0x80 (2-bit) or entry_id >= 16 with depth 0x40 (4-bit), causing OOB write.

Now, how does dvbsubdec parse packets? Let's look at the main decode function. The DVB sub decoder expects data starting with 0x0f 0x0f (data identifier and data unit length?) Actually the DVB subtitle format: each packet starts with data_identifier (0x20-0xFF), then subtitle_stream_id (0x10?), then data units: each data unit has data_unit_id (1 byte), data_unit_length (1 byte), then data. Segment data starts with 0x0f (sync byte), segment_type, page_id (2 bytes), segment_length (2 bytes), then segment data.

Let me look at dvbsub_parse function in dvbsubdec.c.

